**What the mechanism establishes.**

{% if domain_map %}The two {{ domain_map.gene }} alleles are not equivalent, and the analysis treats them separately rather than collapsing them into "biallelic loss". One truncates {{ domain_map.protein }} before the kinase domain begins; the other falls inside that domain. {% endif %}{% if rt %}The truncating allele is {{ rt.nmd_verdict }}, so the transcript a readthrough agent would act on is itself a target of degradation. {% endif %}{% if aneu %}Downstream, the consequence that defines the syndrome was measured rather than assumed: no arm-level aneuploidy across {{ aneu.arms_tested }} arms, with the detection limit of every arm reported, which is a bounded negative and, for a burden spread across many chromosomes at low level, the expected one. {% endif %}Three premises behind this picture are inferences rather than measurements — that the two variants lie in trans, that both were inherited, and that the missense allele is a hypomorph rather than a null — and each is stated as such where it is used and in the Limitations.

**Why repurposing here is compensation, not replacement.**

{% if tract and tract.found %}{{ tract.gene }} is {% if tract.is_essential %}essential and {% endif %}carries no approved drug, no advanced-clinical and no Phase 1 candidate in any modality. {% endif %}The modality with the strongest positive evidence beyond ligandability is degradation, which is the exact inverse of what a loss-of-function disorder needs, and the drugged neighbourhood of the spindle checkpoint consists almost entirely of inhibitors developed to *reduce* mitotic fidelity in dividing tumour cells. A repurposing hypothesis for this child therefore cannot act on the broken protein. Every axis in the candidate section above acts somewhere else: restoring full-length protein from the nonsense allele, reducing the cellular cost of the resulting aneuploidy, or treating the malignancy the syndrome produced. That constraint is enforced mechanically rather than by intention — the anti-target rule excludes any candidate whose mechanism of action inhibits a gene annotated to mitotic fidelity, and it reports what it rejects.

**Which axis the evidence best supports.**

This report groups candidates by axis and by how well each is supported for this child; it does not rank them into a single ordered list, and no candidate is nominated as a preferred treatment. Tier records confidence in a hypothesis, not a clinical preference, and every candidate here is supported by the mechanism or the syndrome rather than by a measurement in this child.

{% if rt %}At the level of axes, the evidence is strongest for readthrough, for one reason: it is the only axis computed from this child's own genome rather than reasoned from the syndrome. The stop codon was read from the genome and confirmed against the transcript annotation ({{ rt.codon_region }}); it is {{ rt.stop_codon }}, the {{ rt.stop_permissiveness }} of the three. The three near-cognate products a readthrough agent would actually produce were each scored, and the residue they would occupy was characterised in its own right — {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_benign }} of the {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_substitutions_scored }} substitutions scored at that position are benign, and the position sits in the {{ rt.residue_region }} rather than inside a folded domain. {% if readthrough and readthrough.clinvar_pathogenic_alleles and readthrough.clinvar_pathogenic_alleles.pathogenic_total %}{% set cv = readthrough.clinvar_pathogenic_alleles %}The axis also reaches past this child: {{ cv.truncating_pct }}% of the pathogenic and likely-pathogenic alleles recorded for this gene are nonsense or frameshift, so the approach applies to a substantial share of patients with this disorder rather than to one private variant. {% endif %}{{ '' }}

Two findings from the same analysis argue against it. The base immediately 3' of the stop is {{ rt.plus4_base_rna }}, which ranks {{ rt.plus4_rank }} of {{ rt.plus4_of }} in the published hierarchy of readthrough-permissive contexts, so the local context works against the strategy that the codon identity favours. And because the premature stop is {{ rt.nmd_verdict }}, readthrough alone acts on a transcript the cell is degrading: the mechanism points to readthrough paired with inhibition of nonsense-mediated decay. No drug has been developed or approved for nonsense-mediated-decay inhibition, and an FDA-wide profiling for NMD modulation found most approved drugs have unremarkable effects {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') }}. A handful do inhibit it off-target — amlexanox, the cardiac glycosides {{ cite('[@nickless2014]') }}, 5-azacytidine {{ cite('[@bhuvanagiri2014]') }} and homoharringtonine {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') }} — and the pairing has raised protein from a mutant transcript in patient-derived cells for another nonsense disorder {{ cite('[@amarschwartz2023]') }}. So the combination is not unavailable in principle. What bounds it here is this child: three of those agents are myelosuppressive or cardiotoxic in a patient already carrying both burdens, and all of them suppress a surveillance pathway globally in a cancer-predisposition syndrome, a concern that outlives the choice among them. It does not outlive the choice of modality. Gene-specific antisense inhibition of NMD silences decay of one transcript rather than the pathway, and was combined with a readthrough compound to restore full-length protein from a nonsense allele {{ cite('[@nomakuchi2016]') }}, since carried toward the clinic for one cystic fibrosis allele {{ cite('[@kim2022]') }}; its open problem is tissue delivery, not selectivity. That is outside a brief asking for approved medicines, which is why the axis here is proposed with its pairing characterised rather than asserted, and why the Limitations set out what was not carried and why.

{% endif %}The remaining axes are weaker, and for stated reasons. Proteostasis support acts on a real and documented consequence of aneuploidy ({{ cite('[@-oromendia2012]') }}; {{ cite('[@-donnelly2014]') }}), but there is no evidence for it in mosaic variegated aneuploidy or in any other aneuploidy syndrome, and no proteotoxic-stress biomarker was measured in this child, so it rests on cell biology rather than on anything observed here. The tumour-directed candidates are weakest *for this analysis* rather than in general: each is selected on features of a tumour genotype — fusion status, copy number, homologous-recombination status, aneuploidy score — that germline sequencing cannot supply, and they propose intensifying therapy in a chromosomal-instability syndrome where the same report raises a question about intensity. They are retained with their tier and their reason recorded, because a demoted candidate with its reason is evidence that the framework discriminates.

**What the pharmacogenomic layer contributes.**

Pharmacogenomics is not the deliverable here, and it earns its place for two specific reasons. It gates a candidate: MT-RNR1 decides whether the aminoglycoside members of the readthrough axis are usable at all, and because no caller available for this gene models heteroplasmy, it was genotyped directly from the reads so the answer rests on measured allele fractions at high depth rather than on a diploid genotype model. The gate is open on pharmacogenomic grounds, while a patient-specific renal caution, drawn from a released clinical feature, points the other way; both are reported, and neither is resolved into a recommendation. {% if counterfactual and counterfactual.applicable %}Second, it demonstrates that method changes answers: re-running the same variant data with copy-number detection disabled moves a phenotype call and would attach dose-adjustment or alternative-drug flags to {{ counterfactual.flagged_counterfactual - counterfactual.flagged_observed }} further annotations that this child's genotype does not warrant. That is a claim about rigour in pharmacogenomic calling, and it generalises past this case. {% endif %}{{ '' }}

**What would move any of this forward.**

Each candidate in the candidate section above carries the evidence that would raise its tier, because a tier without that is an opinion. Across the axes, four pieces of evidence would change the most. A readthrough measurement in a context matching this allele's, since the published hierarchy predicts this one is unfavourable. A measurement of full-length {{ domain_map.protein if domain_map else 'protein' }} restored in patient-derived cells, which would convert the readthrough axis from predicted to observed and would also settle whether the missense allele retains activity. Genotyping the parents at the two variant positions, which would resolve both the phase and the inheritance assumptions that the mechanism section depends on. And, for the tumour-directed axis alone, the tumour's own genotype, without which those candidates cannot be assessed at all.

**Conclusion.**

From one germline genome this analysis derives a mechanism specific enough to constrain what a drug could do, and proposes candidates that follow from that mechanism rather than from the disease name: {% if ot_n_repurposing %}{{ ot_n_repurposing }} approved medicines across {{ ot_by_axis | length }} axes, {% endif %}each grouped by how well it is supported for this child, each tiered with the evidence that would raise it, and each filtered by a rule that refuses any drug that would deepen the mitotic defect. The strongest axis is the one the genome itself speaks to, and the same analysis that supports it reports the two findings that qualify it and the approved-medicine gap that currently limits it.
